## Credit Card Probability of Default {#sec-credit-card}

The first empirical study addresses a concrete question: can GP and DGP classifiers serve as credible probability-of-default (PD) models under realistic credit-risk conditions, and how does their performance scale with training set size? The benchmark used is the UCI default of credit card clients dataset [@yeh_comparisons_2009], comprising 30,000 observations and 23 predictor variables — credit limit, demographics, six months of repayment-status history, and corresponding billing and repayment amounts from April–September 2005. The target indicates default on the October 2005 payment. This benchmark is well-established in the credit-risk literature [@thomas_survey_2000; @huang_using_2011; @mahajan_gaussian_2022] and presents two simultaneous evaluation criteria that are especially revealing for probabilistic models: ranking quality (does the model correctly order clients by default risk?) and probability calibration (are the predicted default probabilities credible estimates of observed default frequencies?). Under the Basel IRB Advanced Approach, both criteria carry legal and capital weight [@basel_committee_basel_2011; @eba_gl_2017_16].

### Data Preparation and Experimental Design

Pre-processing followed the conventions established in the experimental pipeline. Undocumented education and marriage codes were collapsed into an "other" category; skewed monetary variables (bill amounts and repayment amounts) were log-transformed to reduce leverage from extreme values; categorical variables were one-hot encoded; and all continuous predictors were standardised to zero mean and unit variance before model fitting. The full 30,000-observation dataset was first split into a fixed held-out test set of 6,000 observations (20%), stratified by the target variable. The remaining 24,000 observations form the development pool, from which training and validation subsets are drawn. This design ensures that all model configurations across size tiers are evaluated on the identical test set, enabling fair comparison across training budgets.

Three training-set size tiers were evaluated to study the effect of data quantity on model performance:

- **Small** ($N_{\text{dev}} = 500$): 425 training, 75 validation observations.
- **Medium** ($N_{\text{dev}} = 5{,}000$): 4,250 training, 750 validation observations.
- **Large** ($N_{\text{dev}} = 24{,}000$): 20,400 training, 3,600 validation observations (full development set).

The validation split (15% of dev) is used exclusively for XGBoost early stopping; all other models train on the full train+validation pool after hyperparameter selection, following the convention for small-data GP experiments in @hensman_gaussian_2013. The test set default rate is 22.1% across all tiers.

### Model Set and Training Configuration {#sec-uci-models}

The model set comprises three classical tabular baselines and three GP-family models:

- **Logistic Regression** with `class_weight="balanced"` — the standard imbalance-aware linear baseline.
- **Random Forest** with 500 trees and `class_weight="balanced_subsample"` — a bagging ensemble that provides implicit variance reduction.
- **XGBoost** with `scale_pos_weight` — a gradient-boosted tree ensemble with early stopping tuned on the validation set, then refitted on train+validation at the best iteration count.
- **SVGP** — sparse variational GP with 512 inducing points, 150 training epochs, and variational ELBO [@hensman_gaussian_2013].
- **DSVGP-2L** — two-layer deep sparse variational GP with doubly stochastic inference [@salimbeni_doubly_2017], 128 inducing points per layer, hidden dimension 5.
- **DSVGP-3L** — three-layer variant of the above.

All classical models use class-weight rebalancing rather than post-hoc calibration. GP and DGP models receive no explicit class-weight adjustment; the Bernoulli likelihood with sigmoid link provides a natural probabilistic treatment of the binary outcome.

An essential methodological note concerns decision thresholds. Reporting F1 and classification metrics at a fixed $0.5$ cutoff is a common but misleading convention in imbalanced settings [@niculescu_mizil_predicting_2005]: GP and DGP classifiers route probability mass away from the tails (the posterior mean passed through a sigmoid link has limited magnitude), so a $0.5$ threshold systematically penalises them relative to classical baselines with wider score ranges. All threshold-dependent metrics below are therefore reported at each model's F1-optimal threshold alongside the fixed-threshold results.

### Results at Full Scale (Large Tier) {#sec-uci-large}

| Model | ROC-AUC | Avg Prec | F1 ($t = 0.5$) | ECE | Brier | Log Loss | Time (s) |
|:------|:-------:|:--------:|:--------------:|:---:|:-----:|:--------:|:--------:|
| Logistic Regression | 0.739 | 0.493 | 0.500 | 0.225 | 0.199 | 0.590 | 0.04 |
| Random Forest | 0.769 | 0.549 | 0.532 | 0.087 | 0.146 | 0.460 | 5.53 |
| XGBoost | **0.779** | **0.553** | **0.536** | 0.196 | 0.177 | 0.539 | 0.58 |
| SVGP | 0.776 | 0.551 | 0.472 | **0.015** | **0.136** | **0.432** | 143.7 |
| DSVGP-2L | 0.778 | 0.548 | 0.480 | 0.017 | **0.135** | **0.430** | 168.2 |
| DSVGP-3L | 0.777 | 0.546 | 0.488 | 0.016 | 0.136 | 0.433 | 582.8 |

: Principal six-model comparison at full scale ($N_{\text{train}} = 20{,}400$, $N_{\text{test}} = 6{,}000$). Bold entries indicate leading values. {#tbl-uci-large}

::: {layout-ncol=2}
![ROC and precision-recall curves at full scale. XGBoost narrowly leads on ROC-AUC (0.779); GP-family models form the second group at 0.776–0.778.](/sections/4-modeling/assets/credit-card-default/large/roc_pr.png){#fig-uci-large-roc-pr}

![Reliability diagrams at full scale. GP-family models track the diagonal closely (ECE $\leq 0.017$); logistic regression and XGBoost show substantial miscalibration (ECE $> 0.19$).](/sections/4-modeling/assets/credit-card-default/large/calibration.png){#fig-uci-large-calibration}
:::

#### Discriminative Performance {#sec-uci-large-discrimination}

On threshold-independent ranking metrics, the six models form three tiers. XGBoost leads at ROC-AUC = 0.779 and average precision = 0.553, consistent with its strong performance on structured tabular data. The GP family follows closely: DSVGP-2L = 0.778, DSVGP-3L = 0.777, SVGP = 0.776 — a gap of $\Delta = 0.001$–$0.003$ from XGBoost that is well within typical variance for this dataset size. Random forest (0.769) and logistic regression (0.739) trail, the latter limited by its inability to capture the non-linear interactions between repayment-status history and monetary variables that dominate the feature-importance rankings.

At the fixed $0.5$ threshold, XGBoost leads on F1 (0.536) while SVGP reaches only 0.472 — a gap that is a score-distribution artefact rather than a discriminative difference, as noted above. At F1-optimal thresholds, the gap narrows considerably: SVGP achieves F1 = 0.546 at threshold 0.30, DSVGP-2L = 0.541 at 0.30, and XGBoost = 0.543 at 0.55. The +15.7% relative F1 gain for SVGP from threshold optimisation versus +1.3% for XGBoost is a measurement correction that reflects the characteristic narrower score distribution of GP posteriors passed through the sigmoid link.

#### Probability Calibration {#sec-uci-large-calibration}

The principal advantage of GP and DGP classifiers on this dataset is probability calibration. SVGP achieves ECE = 0.015 and Brier = 0.136; DSVGP-3L: ECE = 0.016, Brier = 0.136; DSVGP-2L: ECE = 0.017, Brier = 0.135. Classical baselines are substantially worse: logistic regression ECE = 0.225, XGBoost ECE = 0.196, random forest ECE = 0.087. The GP models achieve approximately **12$\times$ lower ECE** than XGBoost and **13$\times$ lower** than logistic regression.

This pattern aligns with @niculescu_mizil_predicting_2005, who show empirically that gradient-boosted ensembles are systematically overconfident while sigmoid-linked probabilistic models tend toward well-calibrated posteriors. The GP models are well-calibrated natively because the variational ELBO penalises over-confident posteriors and the Bernoulli-sigmoid link ensures appropriate uncertainty near the decision boundary [@hensman_gaussian_2013].

Random forest occupies an intermediate position (ECE = 0.087): its bagging mechanism provides some calibration smoothing, but the frequency-based probability estimates from leaf nodes are still systematically biased relative to the Bayesian posterior of the GP family [@niculescu_mizil_predicting_2005].

Under the Basel IRB Advanced Approach, PD estimates must track observed default rates across rating grades [@eba_gl_2017_16; @basel_committee_basel_2011]. A raw ECE of 0.196 (XGBoost) implies average deviations of nearly 20 percentage points per quantile bin — a level that would fail a standard supervisory calibration review and distort IFRS 9 expected credit loss provisions [@iasb_ifrs9_2014]. GP models satisfy this standard natively; classical models would require a post-hoc calibration stage (Platt scaling [@platt_probabilistic_1999] or isotonic regression [@zadrozny_transforming_2002]) that adds to both the data budget and the validation surface area under model risk frameworks such as SR 11-7 [@federal_reserve_sr11_7].

